7. KEY TECHNICAL CONCEPTS DEMONSTRATED
================================================================================

1. EDGE DETECTION INPUT HANDLING
   Unlike Flappy Bird's continuous "is pressed" check, this game uses 
   proper edge detection (currentButtonState == HIGH && lastButtonState 
   == LOW) to register exactly ONE action per physical button press, 
   regardless of how long the button is held down.

2. ROUND-BASED / TURN-BASED GAME STRUCTURE
   Unlike the continuous real-time gameplay of Flappy Bird, this game 
   uses a distinct ROUND -> RESULT -> NEXT ROUND cycle, demonstrating a 
   different game architecture pattern useful for many other game types 
   (quiz games, puzzle games, etc.)

3. TIMED STATE TRANSITIONS USING millis()
   The showResult state uses millis() - resultTime > 800 to create a 
   non-blocking 800ms pause, allowing the display to show feedback text 
   without freezing the whole program (avoiding delay(800) which would 
   block button reads).

4. DISTANCE-BASED SCORING ALGORITHM
   Score is not simply pass/fail - it's calculated based on proximity 
   to the zone's center, rewarding precision over just "good enough" 
   accuracy. This teaches a more nuanced scoring system design.

5. LIVES SYSTEM VS. INSTANT GAME OVER
   Unlike Flappy Bird (single mistake = game over), this game allows 
   multiple mistakes (3 lives) before ending, demonstrating a different 
   difficulty/forgiveness balance suitable for precision-based games.

6. STREAK/COMBO TRACKING WITH PERSISTENT BEST RECORD
   Two related but distinct variables (streak vs bestStreak) demonstrate 
   how to track both a "current run" value that resets on failure, and 
   an "all-time best" value that persists across resets within a session.

7. DIFFICULTY SCALING TIED TO SKILL (STREAK) RATHER THAN TIME/SCORE
   Difficulty increases based on consecutive successes (streak % 5) 
   rather than total score or elapsed time, meaning skilled players who 
   maintain streaks face harder challenges faster, while players who 
   miss often stay at an easier difficulty for longer - a form of 
   dynamic difficulty adjustment.

